        {
          "id": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3",
          "block_type": "SectionHeader",
          "html": "<h2>♥<b>3.2 Aside: Even Faster Fibonacci Numbers</b></h2>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              63.37820498642988,
              234.6174717135445
            ],
            [
              326.2532958984375,
              234.6174717135445
            ],
            [
              326.2532958984375,
              250.2630615234375
            ],
            [
              63.37820498642988,
              250.2630615234375
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            63.37820498642988,
            234.6174717135445,
            326.2532958984375,
            250.2630615234375
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Text/4",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Text\">Although the previous algorithm is simple and attractive, it is <i>not</i> the fastest algorithm to compute Fibonacci numbers. We can derive a faster algorithm by exploiting the following matrix reformulation of the Fibonacci recurrence:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              69.35089714085889,
              260.3703291904371
            ],
            [
              429.0454866637709,
              260.3703291904371
            ],
            [
              429.0454866637709,
              301.78057861328125
            ],
            [
              69.35089714085889,
              301.78057861328125
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            69.35089714085889,
            260.3703291904371,
            429.0454866637709,
            301.78057861328125
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Equation/5",
          "block_type": "Equation",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Equation\"><math display=\"block\">\\begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\\\ 1 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} y \\\\ x+y \\end{bmatrix}</math></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              195.18619215803605,
              308.51370295465705
            ],
            [
              302.91505282625997,
              308.51370295465705
            ],
            [
              302.91505282625997,
              341.56817626953125
            ],
            [
              195.18619215803605,
              341.56817626953125
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            195.18619215803605,
            308.51370295465705,
            302.91505282625997,
            341.56817626953125
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Text/6",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Text\">In other words, multiplying a two-dimensional vector by the matrix -0 1 1 1 has exactly the same effect as one iteration of the inner loop of IterFibo2. It follows that multiplying by the matrix <i>n</i> times is the same as iterating the loop <i>n</i> times:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              69.83800258606811,
              348.46096395077194
            ],
            [
              429.2715504410478,
              348.46096395077194
            ],
            [
              429.2715504410478,
              391.46002197265625
            ],
            [
              69.83800258606811,
              391.46002197265625
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            69.83800258606811,
            348.46096395077194,
            429.2715504410478,
            391.46002197265625
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Equation/7",
          "block_type": "Equation",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Equation\"><math display=\"block\">\\begin{bmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 \\\\ 1 &amp; 1 \\end{bmatrix}^n \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} F_{n-1} \\\\ F_n \\end{bmatrix}.</math></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              193.63872360775295,
              398.5235509738116
            ],
            [
              303.00146484375,
              398.5235509738116
            ],
            [
              303.00146484375,
              430.75604248046875
            ],
            [
              193.63872360775295,
              430.75604248046875
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            193.63872360775295,
            398.5235509738116,
            303.00146484375,
            430.75604248046875
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Text/8",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Text\">So if we want the <i>n</i>th Fibonacci number, we only need to compute the <i>n</i>th power of the matrix -0 1 1 1 . If we use repeated squaring,<sup>9</sup> computing the <i>n</i>th power of something requires only <i>O</i>(log <i>n</i>) multiplications. Here, because \"something\" is <sup>a</sup> 2 <sup>×</sup> 2 matrix, that means <i>O</i>(log <i>n</i>) 2 <sup>×</sup> 2 matrix multiplications, each of which reduces to a constant number of integer multiplications and additions. Thus, we can compute <i>F</i><i><sup>n</sup></i> in only <i>O</i><b>(</b><b>log</b><i>n</i><b>)</b> <i>integer arithmetic operations</i>.</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              69.44178475339898,
              438.4394893625576
            ],
            [
              429.9038976310386,
              438.4394893625576
            ],
            [
              429.9038976310386,
              522.9741516113281
            ],
            [
              69.44178475339898,
              522.9741516113281
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            69.44178475339898,
            438.4394893625576,
            429.9038976310386,
            522.9741516113281
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Text/9",
          "block_type": "Text",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Text\">We can achieve the same speedup using the identity <i>F</i><i><sup>n</sup></i> = <i>F</i><i>m</i><i>F</i><i>n</i>−<i>m</i>−<sup>1</sup> + <i>F</i><i>m</i>+1<i>F</i><i>n</i>−<i>m</i>, which holds (by induction!) for all integers <i>m</i> and <i>n</i>. In particular, this identity implies the following mutual recurrence for pairs of adjacent Fibonacci numbers, first proposed by Édouard Lucas in 1898:</p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              69.79537256694692,
              520.2879563287175
            ],
            [
              429.6175776314031,
              520.2879563287175
            ],
            [
              429.6175776314031,
              574.9395980834961
            ],
            [
              69.79537256694692,
              574.9395980834961
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            69.79537256694692,
            520.2879563287175,
            429.6175776314031,
            574.9395980834961
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "/page/0/Equation/10",
          "block_type": "Equation",
          "html": "<p block-type=\"Equation\"><math display=\"block\">F_{2n-1} = F_{n-1}^2 + F_n^2</math>\n<math display=\"block\">F_{2n} = F_n(F_{n-1} + F_{n+1}) = F_n(2F_{n-1} + F_n)</math></p>",
          "polygon": [
            [
              151.49020523379787,
              582.2646198706664
            ],
            [
              345.7559916020181,
              582.2646198706664
            ],
            [
              345.7559916020181,
              621.4018402099609
            ],
            [
              151.49020523379787,
              621.4018402099609
            ]
          ],
          "bbox": [
            151.49020523379787,
            582.2646198706664,
            345.7559916020181,
            621.4018402099609
          ],
          "children": null,
          "section_hierarchy": {
            "2": "/page/0/SectionHeader/3"
          },
          "images": {}
        },
